Architecture Proposal: SurrealDB as the "Slow Control" & Provenance Plane

1. Executive Summary

The rudaq codebase already implements a highly optimized, zero-copy data plane utilizing a lock-free Pool<T> and Bytes-backed frames. Furthermore, it safely broadcasts telemetry via SystemStateSnapshot at 30Hz and isolates metadata inside EventDoc.

Because the "fast path" is already effectively solved, SurrealDB should not be inserted into the high-frequency telemetry or data loops. Instead, SurrealDB will act as the durable "Slow Control" and Provenance Plane. It will manage hierarchical hardware configurations, multi-run experiment graphs, and durable job queues, sitting strictly above the existing real-time Tokio layer.

2. The Delineation of Responsibilities

2.1 The Data Plane (Existing - Do Not Change)

Zero-Copy Execution: crates/pool/src/buffer_pool.rs continues to handle PooledBuffer allocations.

Fast Telemetry: crates/common/src/state_cache.rs continues its 30Hz SystemStateSnapshot loop.

Payload Isolation: EventDoc continues to carry scalars (HashMap<String, f64>) and small arrays (≤64KB), while large binary blobs go directly to crates/storage.

2.2 The Slow Control Plane (SurrealDB)

SurrealDB takes ownership of system persistence, bridging the gap between volatile daemon lifecycles and permanent storage.

Hierarchical Configuration: Replaces static TOML files with a queryable database of device capabilities and user configurations.

Relationship Graphing: Maps relationships seamlessly: User -> Experiment -> Scan Intent -> Run UID -> Zarr File.

Audit Trailing: Provides a durable, queryable history of state transitions (e.g., tracking why ShutterRegistry::emergency_close_all() was called and by which script).

3. Implementation Strategy: The Persistence Bridge

We introduce an asynchronous SurrealSink that consumes the existing SystemStateSnapshot and EventDoc broadcasts, batch-inserting them into SurrealDB for provenance without blocking the DAQ threads.

3.1 SurrealDB Schema Overview

-- Devices table acts as the permanent digital twin
DEFINE TABLE device SCHEMAFULL;
DEFINE FIELD identifier ON device TYPE string;
DEFINE FIELD last_known_state ON device TYPE string;
DEFINE FIELD capabilities ON device TYPE object;

-- Provenance Graph
DEFINE TABLE experiment SCHEMAFULL;
DEFINE TABLE run SCHEMAFULL;
DEFINE TABLE event_metadata SCHEMAFULL;

-- Graph Edges
DEFINE TABLE CONTAINS TYPE RELATION FROM experiment TO run;
DEFINE TABLE GENERATED TYPE RELATION FROM run TO event_metadata;
